gence.\n    *   The Project GROOT system is also represented by a green cube with a neural network diagram inside, similar to the Tokkio system.\n    *   The output of the Project GROOT system is an action that is executed by the robot.\n*   **Action**\n    *   The action is represented by a yellow gear icon, symbolizing the physical movement or task performed by the robot.\n    *   The action is the result of the processing done by the NVIDIA Project GROOT system.\n*   **Robot**\n    *   The robot is represented by a green icon with a robotic body, indicating its mechanical nature.\n    *   The robot receives the action output from the Project GROOT system and executes it accordingly.\n\nIn summary, the flowchart illustrates the seamless integration of human input, AI processing, and robotic execution, highlighting the potential of NVIDIA's Project GROOT to revolutionize the field of robotics. By leveraging advanced AI models like Tokkio and Project GROOT, robots can be trained to perform complex tasks with precision and accuracy, opening up new possibilities for applications in various industries.", 'similarity score': 0.6041828393936157}, {'frames/frame_6704': 'frames/frame_6704', 'text': 'The image shows a side-by-side comparison of a robot in a digital environment and a real-life robot in a workshop.', 'similarity score': 0.60329270362854}, {'6258': '6258', 'text': 'frames/frame_6264 seconds: The image presents a comprehensive overview of NVIDIA\'s robotics platform adoption, highlighting its widespread use and versatility in various applications. \n\n*   The left side of the image features a graphic illustrating the adoption statistics, with a title that reads "NVIDIA Robotics Platform Adoption" and a statistic indicating 1.3 million robotics developers, 100,000 ROS developers, and 6,000 companies developing on Orin.\n    *   The statistic is displayed prominently in green text, accompanied by a visual representation of various robots and robotic components.\n